       <h1>Bangladesh Drafts Uncapped Aliss Al Islam for Asian Games Following Saifuddin Injury</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Bangladesh</span> <span>#Asian Games</span> <span>#Player Injury</span></div>
       <p>The Bangladesh Cricket Board (BCB) confirmed a significant squad change for the upcoming Asian Games on September 23, 2026, bringing uncapped spinner Aliss Al Islam into the fold. This development comes after seasoned all-rounder Mohammad Saifuddin was regrettably ruled out of the tournament. Saifuddin sustained a right hamstring injury during a practice session, a setback that will undoubtedly be felt by the team aiming for success on the continental stage.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-23/bangladesh-drafts-uncapped-aliss-al-islam-for-asian-games-fo.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Aliss Al Islam's call-up represents a considerable opportunity for the young player to make his international debut in a high-profile event. While the specifics of his role in the squad will unfold, his inclusion highlights the BCB's readiness to tap into fresh talent when faced with unforeseen challenges. Saifuddin's absence leaves a void, particularly given his all-round capabilities, but it also opens the door for Aliss Al Islam to showcase his skills and potentially cement a place in future national squads. The team management will now be strategizing how best to integrate the new face and adapt their game plan for the demanding competition.</p><p>What to watch: How Aliss Al Islam integrates into the squad and performs on debut.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately reflects the source without introducing unbacked facts.</p>
