       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · BBC Health · 2026-09-23 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Fresh bedsheets and dusk walks: Expert tips for sleep‑deprived freshers</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#sleep</span> <span>#freshers</span> <span>#students</span> <span>#evening walk</span></div>
       <p>University freshers struggling with irregular sleep patterns are being urged to adopt simple lifestyle tweaks that can improve rest quality. Health experts recommend swapping out old bedding for fresh, clean sheets, noting that a crisp sleeping environment can signal the body to unwind. An evening stroll at dusk is also highlighted as a low‑intensity activity that helps regulate the circadian rhythm by exposing the eyes to natural light before darkness sets in. The guidance stresses consistency: going to bed and waking at the same times each day, limiting screen exposure an hour before sleep, and avoiding caffeine in the late afternoon. Freshers are reminded that academic pressures often lead to late‑night study sessions, but building a routine that incorporates a brief walk and a tidy bedroom can reduce sleep debt. While the advice is general, it aligns with broader research linking environmental cues and moderate physical activity to deeper, more restorative sleep. Implementing these steps may help students maintain focus and academic performance throughout the semester.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-23/fresh-bedsheets-and-dusk-walks-expert-tips-for-sleepdeprived.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>What to watch: adoption rates of the simple sleep‑hygiene habits among campuses.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article elaborates reasonably on the source's core advice for student sleep hygiene.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmwyz2p41ezvo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC Health</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
