     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-09-23 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India’s Cheetah Count Reaches 56 as KGP12 Gives Birth to 4 Cubs at Kuno</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Wildlife</span> <span>#Conservation</span> <span>#Cheetahs</span> <span>#India</span></div>
       <p>KGP12, an India-born cheetah, has given birth to four cubs at Kuno National Park on September 18, a significant milestone just after Project Cheetah completed four years. This new litter elevates India's living cheetah population to 56, with 36 of these magnificent animals now born within the country.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-23/indias-cheetah-count-reaches-56-as-kgp12-gives-birth-to-4-cu.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This event marks a poignant new chapter for KGP12. She previously gave birth to four cubs in April 2026, but tragically, that first litter did not survive, succumbing to a leopard attack in the wild within weeks. This time, the new cubs were born inside a secured enclosure at Kuno, offering enhanced protection during their vulnerable early stages, with forest officials monitoring from a distance to minimize disturbance.</p><p>KGP12 herself was born at Kuno in 2024 to Gamini, a cheetah brought from South Africa, making her part of the first generation of cheetahs reintroduced to India. Her new offspring represent the vital next generation, crucial for establishing a self-sustaining population. Project Cheetah, which began with cheetahs from Namibia in September 2022, and later from South Africa and Botswana, aims for long-term survival and reproduction in India.</p><p>Why it matters: This birth signifies a critical step towards establishing a resilient, self-propagating cheetah population in India, proving the success of reintroduction efforts.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article faithfully captures the key points of the cheetah story.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/wildlife/india-born-cheetah-kgp12-four-cubs-kuno-first-litter-becoming-mother-again-conservation-effort-12558472" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
