     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · India Today · 2026-09-23 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>SIR row: What changed in Form 6, and why did 2 Election Commissioners object?</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national news</span> <span>#election commission</span> <span>#Form 6</span> <span>#controversy</span></div>
       <p>A national controversy, widely referred to as the &quot;SIR row,&quot; has centered on reported changes to Form 6 and subsequent objections voiced by two Election Commissioners. While the existence of this dispute is acknowledged, specific details regarding the exact modifications made to Form 6 have not been publicly disclosed.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-23/sir-row-what-changed-in-form-6-and-why-did-2-election-commis.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The core of the matter involves an inquiry into what precisely was altered within Form 6. This document is typically integral to electoral procedures, often related to voter registration or other foundational aspects of the election process. Without explicit information on these changes, the public and political observers are left with questions about their potential impact on future elections.</p><p>Furthermore, the reasons behind the objections from two Election Commissioners remain unspecified. Their dissent underscores an internal disagreement within the electoral body, signaling that the proposed or implemented changes are of sufficient concern to warrant formal opposition from high-ranking officials. The nature of their concerns, whether procedural, ethical, or legal, has not been detailed in available reports.</p><p>The ongoing &quot;SIR row&quot; highlights a demand for transparency regarding electoral processes. Clarification from relevant authorities on both the changes to Form 6 and the Election Commissioners' objections will be essential for addressing public and political scrutiny and upholding confidence in the electoral system.</p><p>What to watch: Further official statements on the Form 6 changes and Election Commissioner objections.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Appropriately handles the minimal source information by noting the lack of specific details without fabricating facts.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.indiatoday.in/india/story/ecinet-form-6-controversy-election-commissioners-object-voter-roll-changes-3001446-2026-09-23?utm_source=rss" rel="noopener">India Today</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
