     <article class="story single" data-beat="health">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · BBC Health · 2026-09-23 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Student almost dies after accidental paracetamol overdose</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Health</span> <span>#Paracetamol overdose</span> <span>#Medication safety</span> <span>#Organ failure</span></div>
       <p>A student recently experienced severe health complications, including organ failure, following an accidental overdose of paracetamol. The incident led to a near-fatal outcome, prompting the student's parents to speak out about the distressing experience.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-23/student-almost-dies-after-accidental-paracetamol-overdose.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The parents conveyed their profound distress over the situation and expressed bewilderment regarding the rapid onset of the severe symptoms. They stated they had &quot;no idea how it happened so quickly,&quot; highlighting the unexpected and swift deterioration of their child's health.</p><p>Accidental paracetamol overdoses can lead to serious health issues, particularly affecting the liver, and in severe cases, can result in organ failure. This incident serves as a stark reminder of the potential dangers associated with improper medication use, even with commonly available over-the-counter drugs.</p><p>The family's experience underscores the importance of adhering strictly to dosage instructions for all medications. Even a slight deviation can have significant, life-threatening consequences, especially with drugs like paracetamol where the therapeutic dose is relatively close to a toxic dose.</p><p>While the specific circumstances leading to this accidental overdose were not detailed, the parents' account emphasizes the critical need for awareness regarding medication safety.</p><p>What to watch: Public health campaigns and educational initiatives aimed at preventing accidental medication overdoses.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately conveys the sentiments and details provided in the source summary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cq0kly26dykmo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC Health</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
