     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-09-23 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>This $199 GPS sports watch is repairable, modular, and open-source</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tech</span> <span>#Wearables</span> <span>#Repairability</span> <span>#Open Source</span></div>
       <p>A new GPS sports watch, the Una Watch, is making headlines for its distinctive design philosophy and competitive pricing. Initially launched through Kickstarter at $350, the device is now available for $199, positioning it below the cost of many established Garmin models. This price adjustment makes the Una Watch a notable contender in the crowded sports wearable market.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-23/this-199-gps-sports-watch-is-repairable-modular-and-open-sou.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Beyond its affordability, the Una Watch distinguishes itself with a modular, repairable, and open-source design. This approach stands in contrast to the often sealed and proprietary nature of many consumer electronics, offering users greater control over their device's longevity and customization. The emphasis on repairability is a key selling point, potentially appealing to a growing segment of environmentally conscious consumers and tech enthusiasts.</p><p>Technical specifications for the Una Watch include a 1.2-inch MIP LCD display, a common choice for sports watches due to its excellent visibility in direct sunlight and power efficiency. The device also boasts an impressive battery life, capable of lasting up to 10 days on a single charge. This endurance is comparable to models such as the Garmin Forerunner 70, suggesting strong performance for extended athletic activities or daily use without frequent recharging.</p><p>The combination of a reduced price point and a user-centric design philosophy positions the Una Watch as an intriguing option for consumers. Its modularity and open-source nature could foster a community of users who can repair or even enhance their devices, potentially extending their lifespan beyond typical consumer electronics.</p><p>What to watch: How the open-source and repairable aspects of the Una Watch influence its market adoption and community development.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Faithfully mirrors the source information; no extraneous claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
